      "value": "To expand on this a bit after seeing some confusion in discussions on discord about why this is needed - this fundamentally boils down to needing a way to help different UdonBehaviours and GameObjects to cooperate and share the limited bandwidth available in a fair manner.\nIn another Canny, there was a note that a getIsClogged will be added. This helps a bit, but it doesn't quite help enough. Consider if we have two UdonBehaviours that both want to send data. UdonBehaviour A checks getIsClogged, sees that it returns false, and proceeds to transmit. The network becomes clogged, and UdonBehaviour B sees that and refrains from transmitting. Later, the clog clears, but again UdonBehaviour A checks first, and transmits again (perhaps its timer for checking is shorter than B?). B never gets a chance to transmit. This is further complicated if there are multiple UBs on the same GameObject as well. This kind of pathological behavior can only be avoided with some kind of central control of when different GameObjects get a chance to transmit.\nExplicitly setting a throttle in user code doesn't help either, because the network bandwidth cap is subject to change, the amount of bandwidth used by a sync operation is nontrivial to compute, and because if we set a throttle in a prefab for distribution, the value that throttle should have depends on what else is in the world and what it's doing at the time.\nFinally, many prefab authors aren't going to be experts in network congestion control, and if you ask them to implement congestion control algorithms, they will get it wrong, guaranteed (but it'll probably work okay when they're testing in isolation!).\nMy proposal here is for a minimal change to help ensure that 1) UdonBehaviours share bandwidth in a fair manner, 2) latency doesn't get out of hand and 3) in most cases, prefab authors don't need to think about congestion control. This doesn't change existing semantics, because already RequestSerialization doesn't _immediately_ serialize, but waits a bit.\nSimply making sure that Udon always waits for the queued data from a particular GameObject to finish sending before queueing up more ensures a more-or-less round-robin allocation of transmit opportunities among all GameObjects, limits the peak latency of transmission, and is probably what you want anyway for what is probably the majority of use cases where the final state of the synced vars is all that matters.",
      "viewerReactions": null
    },
